### change (ws/sd-3k)
# run 1  worker=94.06   worker_rows=10.63M  worker_cpu=175.97  client=88.57  total=182.63  5.48M
# run 2  worker=96.42   worker_rows=10.37M  worker_cpu=177.05  client=86.98  total=183.41  5.45M
# run 3  worker=95.68   worker_rows=10.45M  worker_cpu=178.32  client=88.62  total=184.30  5.43M
# run 4  worker=94.99   worker_rows=10.53M  worker_cpu=180.71  client=88.45  total=183.44  5.45M
# run 5  worker=96.46   worker_rows=10.37M  worker_cpu=178.20  client=86.72  total=183.18  5.46M
# median worker=95.68 ns/row (10.45M rows/s)  worker_cpu=178.20  client=88.45  total=183.41 ns/row (5.45M rows/s)

### AB (3k rows/fetch)
# baseline -> change: worker 104.85 -> 95.68 ns/row (-8.7%, 9.54M -> 10.45M rows/s, +9.5%)
#                     total  191.08 -> 183.41 ns/row (-4.0%, 5.23M -> 5.45M rows/s, +4.2%)
# base -> change:     worker  98.46 -> 95.68 ns/row (-2.8%, 10.16M -> 10.45M rows/s, +2.9%)
#                     total  187.18 -> 183.41 ns/row (-2.0%, 5.34M -> 5.45M rows/s, +2.1%)
# The price is visible and bounded: worker_cpu 88.44 -> 178.20 ns/row. The spin
# burns a core for at most 300 us per handoff instead of sleeping; on the 3k
# path the partner is working for that whole window, so the wall time falls and
# the CPU is the idle core the read was not using anyway.

## 100K rows/fetch — the control (pipelined DBAPI, batch 100000 > spin gate)

### baseline (main@7e3d2e6, 3 runs, medians)
# DBAPI 3.72M | stream() 8.32M | 4 procs 8.62M | zero-copy stream() 8.42M rows/s

### base (merge/transport @ 303366d, 8 runs)
# DBAPI raw:  8.46 8.01 7.70 7.68 7.02 8.09 7.51 7.70  -> median 7.70M
# stream raw: 9.59 8.93 8.45 8.80 9.34 9.23 8.70 9.84  -> median 9.08M
